---
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Debugging with AI - AI Training Guide">
  <PageShell chapterId="development" subChapterId="debugging">
    <div class="prose space-y-6">
      <p>
        Debugging is one of the areas where AI provides the most immediate value. You do not need to craft a perfect prompt or plan an architecture. You just
        need to give the AI the right information about what went wrong.
      </p>

      <h3>Instrument First, Debug Second</h3>

      <p>
        Before you start chasing a bug, ask the AI to add <code>console.warn</code> and <code>console.error</code> statements or similar logging throughout the relevant code, including intermediate data.
        This is a task AI handles well because it is mechanical and repetitive, and the AI can identify logical branch points where logging is most useful.
      </p>

      <p>
        A prompt like "add console.warn statements to every branch in the checkout flow so I can trace what path is being taken" gives you visibility fast. You
        do not need to understand the bug yet. You just need to see what the code is actually doing.
      </p>

      <p>
        Some tools now run this whole loop for you. Cursor's{" "}
        <a href="https://cursor.com/blog/debug-mode" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Debug Mode</a>{" "}
        generates hypotheses about what is wrong, instruments the code with logging itself, has you reproduce the bug, then reads the logs back to
        pinpoint the root cause before proposing a fix. The manual approach here works in any tool, and understanding it helps you steer the automated one.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-8">
        <div class="text-xs font-bold text-stone-500 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">Example prompt</div>
        <p class="font-mono text-sm text-stone-700 leading-relaxed">
          Add console.warn statements to every function in src/services/payment.ts. Include the function name and all parameter values so I can trace execution
          through the payment flow.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p>Once you reproduce the issue, the logs tell you where the problem is and you can generally just paste the logs into the AI to get it to identify the issue.</p>

      <h3>Pasting Errors Effectively</h3>

      <p>
        When you hit an error, your instinct might be to paste the entire terminal output or full stack trace into the AI. This works, but you get better
        results when you trim it down to the relevant parts. A 200-line build log mostly contains noise. The actual error is usually 5 to 10 lines.
      </p>

      <p>Focus on pasting:</p>

      <ul class="space-y-2 my-4">
        <li>The error message itself</li>
        <li>The stack trace showing where it originated (but only the first few frames)</li>
        <li>Any context about what you were doing when it happened</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        If you are unsure which part matters, paste the full output but tell the AI what you were trying to do. "I ran <code>pnpm build</code> and got this output"
        gives the AI enough framing to find the relevant error in the noise (including the URL if it's a web application).
      </p>

      <Callout variant="tip" title="Practical tip">
        <p>
          When using an AI coding agent like Claude Code or Cursor, the AI can run the code itself and read the errors directly. Instead of copying errors
          manually, let the agent run the failing command so it sees the full output with the right context.
        </p>
      </Callout>

      <h3>Let the Agent Gather the Evidence</h3>

      <p>
        The techniques above assume you are the one running the code and relaying what you see. Increasingly the agent can do that part itself, which removes
        the copy-paste step and the mistakes it introduces.
      </p>

      <ul class="space-y-3 my-4">
        <li>
          <span class="font-semibold">It reads the terminal directly.</span>{" "}
          Agents like Claude Code and Cursor run the failing command and read the full output, so they work from the real error and stack trace rather than
          your paraphrase. Terminal multiplexers built for agents, like{" "}
          <a href="https://herdr.dev" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">herdr</a>, go further and let one agent read another agent's live output.
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="font-semibold">It reads the browser, for web apps.</span>{" "}
          With{" "}
          <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/chrome" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Claude in Chrome</a>{" "}
          or the{" "}
          <a href="https://github.com/ChromeDevTools/chrome-devtools-mcp" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Chrome DevTools MCP</a>{" "}
          server, the agent can read console errors, network requests, and screenshots from a live page, then fix the code that caused them. For UI and
          runtime bugs this beats describing what you saw.
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="font-semibold">It debugs from real errors.</span>{" "}
          Connect a monitoring tool over MCP (Sentry, for example) and the agent can pull an actual production stack trace and work from that instead of a
          reproduction you have to construct.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        None of this takes you out of the loop. The agent is faster at gathering evidence and proposing a fix, but you still confirm the fix is correct and
        that you <a href="/development/owning-the-code">agree with it</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>When the AI Gets Stuck: Search the Docs</h3>

      <p>
        AI models have a knowledge cutoff, and libraries change. If the AI's first suggestion does not fix the problem, ask it to look things up rather than
        continuing to guess. Depending on your tool, this might mean asking it to search the web or read the library's documentation.
      </p>

      <p>
        A prompt like "that didn't work, can you check the docs for this library's latest migration guide?" is often more productive than another round of the
        AI trying variations of the same approach.
      </p>

      <p>
        In agent-based tools like Claude Code, the AI can search the web or read documentation sites directly.
      </p>
    </div>
  </PageShell>
</MainLayout>
